**C1-specific risks:**

- **C1-R1 — First production execution of the
  containment code is read-only, and that cuts both
  ways.** The transition writer paths stay unexecuted
  until C3; a latent defect in a READ path could
  misreport state at §5 V5. Bounded by: the census
  cross-checks (V6/V7 cover facts containment does not
  control), trigger T4 (any containment surface
  misbehavior fires rollback evaluation), and the
  harness battery (§8).
- **C1-R2 — Durable Object runtime adoption is
  UNRESOLVED, and is not established by C1.** Script
  deployment verification (§5) establishes that the new
  script was uploaded byte-exact and that the surfaces
  read during verification answered on it. It does NOT
  establish that every existing DO instance has
  adopted the new code: instances adopt on their own
  lifecycle (eviction, restart, new instantiation),
  the timing is UNSPECIFIED in the reviewed
  documentation, and no C1 check observes per-instance
  adoption. A long-lived instance may keep serving the
  previous build for an unbounded interval after a
  fully verified deploy, and no shipped surface
  reports which build a given instance is serving.
  The D2 read-back, V1's stamp check, and the dual
  observation windows do NOT bound this — they are
  script-level checks. The operational behavior of the
  containment code paths on instances that have adopted
  is likewise unobserved in C1 (C2/C3's subject; C3's
  PARTIAL). Runtime adoption and operational behavior
  therefore remain explicitly unresolved after C1,
  however clean the deploy verification reads; they
  resolve, if at all, only by independent observation
  in later scopes.
- **C1-R3 — The deployed tree is assembled at gate
  time.** The build-pin/stamp convention (§1.3) means
  the exact uploaded bytes exist only from the gate's
  assembly step. Bounded by: hash assertions against
  §1 at assembly, the battery re-run on the assembled
  tree (§8), and the stamped-manifest read-back (V2).

**What C1 does NOT resolve, stated plainly:** whether
containment works operationally (C3, partial); whether
the alarm chain can be revived (R5a-D + S1); why the
alarm stalled (R-09); the recorder blind spot (R-06);
any generation question. After a fully successful C1,
the system differs from today in exactly one respect:
the containment script is deployed and verified as a
script, and its read surfaces report the inert state —
with runtime adoption expressly unresolved (item
C1-R2).

